   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.2",
    "topic_title": "Cell Size",
    "stem": "A cube-shaped cell grows from a side length of 10 µm to a side length of 20 µm. For a cube, surface area = 6s² and volume = s³. Which statement correctly describes how this growth changes the cell's surface area-to-volume ratio and its exchange with the environment?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Surface area and volume both increase 8-fold, so the SA:V ratio stays the same and exchange efficiency is unchanged.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This error assumes that surface area and volume scale together. Surface area depends on s², so doubling the side multiplies it by 4, not 8, and the ratio drops. Fix: Compute surface area and volume separately at both sizes before you compare their ratio."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Surface area increases faster than volume, so the SA:V ratio rises and the larger cell exchanges materials more efficiently.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reverses the exponents. Volume (s³) grows faster than surface area (s²), so the ratio falls as the cell gets larger, and exchange becomes less efficient. Fix: Check which quantity has the higher exponent, since that quantity grows faster as size increases."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Total surface area increases 4-fold, so the cell has more membrane and supplies its cytoplasm more efficiently than before.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses absolute surface area with the surface area-to-volume ratio. Total membrane does increase, but volume increases 8-fold, so each unit of cytoplasm has less membrane serving it. Fix: Ask whether the question concerns total surface area or surface area per unit volume, and then compute SA:V."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Surface area increases 4-fold while volume increases 8-fold, so the SA:V ratio falls from 0.6 to 0.3 µm⁻¹ and each unit of cytoplasm is served by less membrane, making exchange less efficient.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Surface area scales with s² (600 to 2,400 µm²) but volume scales with s³ (1,000 to 8,000 µm³). Volume, which sets the demand for nutrients and waste removal, outpaces the membrane that supplies it. The ratio halves, so exchange per unit volume drops."
     }
    ]
   },
